From 1b8ca2341413ecbe8d2fc2c5f068749d94320b80 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 18:52:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] x86-64: GCC-4.6 ABI for over-aligned MEMORY-class varargs
 (pr92904)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

gcc.c-torture pr92904 aborted 13/30 runs at -O2 on main (ASLR-dependent,
the classic nondeterministic signature). Root cause: GCC >= 4.6 honors
the FULL natural alignment of MEMORY-class stack arguments; the callee's
va_arg overflow walk aligns dynamically from the first stack argument,
but a merely 16-aligned push sequence lands that anchor at rsp mod 32/64
half the time and the callee skips past the argument.

Caller side (x86-64): compute_stack_arg_padding grows an align_cap —
usize::MAX for x86-64 (uncapped per-arg pads, GCC-identical), 16 for
ARM/RISC-V (their psABIs cap stack-argument alignment at 2*XLEN and
their readers cap identically). When any stack argument needs alignment
A > 16, emit_call_stack_args realigns %rsp BEFORE any push by
D = (R0 - TOTAL) mod A (TOTAL = 16-byte save slot + parity pad + static
argument area), so the final (%rsp) at the call — the anchor — lands
exactly on an A boundary; A is a multiple of 16 and TOTAL is
16-rounded, so %rsp stays 16-aligned at the call. The pre-realignment
%rsp is saved in a 16-byte slot ABOVE every argument (the overflow walk
never reads past the last consumed argument) and the cleanup restores
it exactly — correct under DynAlloca. The dynamic delta forbids
%rsp-relative addressing, so generation.rs pins such functions to an
rbp frame (conservative scan of CallInfo.struct_arg_aligns; GCC's own
answer is to realign the whole frame).

Callee side: classify_params_full uncaps the named-param layout the
same way for the SysV classification, so GCC-caller -> lccc-callee
named >16-aligned stack params agree; lccc-caller -> lccc-callee is
consistent by construction.

Validation: pr92904 0/30 aborts (was 13/30); caller/callee split-compile
interop against gcc -O2 in all four directions for aligned(32) and
aligned(64) vararg structs with anchor-parity sweeps 0..20 stack slots,
integer-only overaligned structs, a DynAlloca-before-realigned-call
shape, and the named-param shape — 25/25 everywhere on x86-64 and i686.
Pin: tests/regression/vararg_overalign_call.c.
