From 239c42b83ba97d70608fedff9cabb0c86556bb4d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 18:52:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] aarch64 + riscv: skip dead widening casts after
 Clz/Ctz/Popcount
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Transfer of the x86-64 bitop_nonneg_values discipline (same per-
definition, never-propagated rule): Clz/Ctz/Popcount on <= 32-bit
integer types are in [0, 32] — bit 31 is provably clear.

AArch64: the emitters write the result with W-register instructions,
which zero the upper half of the X register, and sign-extension is the
identity on non-negative values — the sxtw of an I32->I64 widening cast
is dead. A same-register W move is also dead; a cross-register move
zero-extends correctly. The relay fallback loads the 4-byte slot with
ldr w (zeroing) and stores without the extension.

RISC-V: the count is built from 0 upward in a full 64-bit register, so
sext.w is the identity; an lw reload of the stored 4-byte value sign-
extends a non-negative value — equally the identity. The I32->I64 cast
path intercepts before the generic relay.

Verified against the cross GCC oracles: the shape pin
(check_bitop_nonneg_zext.sh) confirms widen_clz/ctz/pop lose the
extension on both targets while the plain-int control keeps it, and a
full value-domain differential (all 32 single-bit inputs + 0 + all-
ones + mixed, clz/ctz/popcount on 32/64-bit, derived arithmetic, and
chained widens) executes clean under qemu for aarch64 and riscv64 and
natively for x86-64.
