From 3676126a9ed3dce3cc2a03c5312c5ab8594ef8ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <m.seyfarth@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 18:52:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] bool_thread: thread int-phi merges tested through a
 merge-local compare
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The vsprintf/printf '--precision >= 0' shape merges an int phi and
tests it through a compare before branching. Threading re-materializes
Cmp(op, v_i, const) on each predecessor's own incoming value; constant
arms fold at compile time. Because this shape duplicates an
instruction, it threads ONLY when every predecessor is threadable (the
merge's death must pay for the compares) and only when the compare's
dest has no use outside the merge's own branch condition.

Two soundness hardenings over the original proposal, both required:

- Bool-shape candidates with a tolerated merge-local Cmp: rule 3 now
  admits a Cmp-LHS use of a merge phi, which would let a candidate
  whose branch tests a PHI delete a merge that still contains a LIVE
  Cmp (dest used after the branch) — the merge's death would dangle
  that use. The bool-shape path therefore rejects the candidate unless
  the compare dest is provably dead (cmp_dest_used_elsewhere). A
  targeted repro (phi + live merge-local Cmp + branch on the phi)
  drives the difference.

- eval_const_cmp returns None for types wider than the i64 carrier
  (I128 phis): masking the carriers to 64 bits would fold high-word
  comparisons incorrectly. None rejects the candidate via the existing
  pre-validation instead.

Validation: 150-program randomized differential against gcc -O0/-O2
(lccc -O0/-O1/-O2 all agreeing) over dead-after-test, live-after-test,
constant-arm, unsigned, loop-carried, critical-edge, and bool-phi-with-
dead-Cmp shapes.
